But I do think it illustrates this point.

235
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:34,080
My wife and I went to a really conservative Baptist college.

236
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:37,900
And what that meant is that we had a curfew of 10 p.m.

237
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:41,480
We could not be out of our dorms later than 10 p.m.

238
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:50,080
And so one night at around 9 p.m., my wife and I decided to go to our Jackson Hall, and we were going to create this birthday sign for our friend.

239
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:51,200
She's like super shy.

240
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:55,340
We're going to paint the sign, and we're going to hang it up, and she's going to be embarrassed, and it was going to be awesome, right?

241
00:13:56,520 --> 00:14:01,760
So we're painting the sign, painting the sign, and it's like 15 minutes until 10 p.m., and we're like pretty much done.

242
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:07,860
And we pull up the sign, and the cybers that I chose for the sign was not sticking off.

243
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:13,620
And so you saw happy birthday, Aaron, on the carpet of the classroom in Jackson Hall.

244
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:16,160
And I was like, okay, this is a problem.

245
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:19,240
So we're going to have to figure this out.

246
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:23,020
And luckily at the time, Marissa worked for the school, so she had a key to the janitor's closet.

247
00:14:23,020 --> 00:14:25,220
And so we decided we're going to take it into our own hands.

248
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:26,160
It's our problem.

249
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:26,880
We created this.

250
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:27,620
We're going to fix it.

251
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:32,720
So we're cleaning and cleaning, and it's taking a while, and it's a struggle.

252
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:41,240
And we get it up, and then we realize it's a little bit after 10, and the security guards are coming through Jackson Hall to lock the building up.

253
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:50,160
And at that point, you know, if I was wise and not a college boy, I would have just, like, went to the security guards and explained, like, hey, this is what happened.

254
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:53,840
You know, we should have talked to you guys, but we're trying to fix the problem.

255
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,340
No, I decided that we were going to run.

256
00:14:55,340 --> 00:15:01,520
So, yeah, like any godly man, you know, I was like, we're going to run.

257
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:02,260
So we ran.

258
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:03,560
They saw us running.

259
00:15:03,560 --> 00:15:04,460
They chased us.

260
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:08,020
And security guards tend to have, like, this, like, bravitas behind them.

261
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:11,060
You know, like the mall cops are like, this is my time.

262
00:15:11,060 --> 00:15:12,400
This is what I was called for.

263
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:15,840
And so they're, like, taking this thing really seriously, this hunt.

264
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:22,560
And so we're running upstairs and hiding and hiding, and then finally I get to the third floor, and, like, I can hear them coming up the steps.

265
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:24,760
And the door in front of me is a handicapped bathroom.

266
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:27,140
And I freeze, panic.

267
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:29,340
I'm like, that's where we're going.

268
00:15:29,560 --> 00:15:34,640
So we both run into the handicapped bathroom and hide around the corner of the stall.

269
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:41,560
And about three minutes later, security guard shines a flashlight on our feet and is like, hey there.

270
00:15:43,060 --> 00:15:45,780
And so here's the truth, here's the facts.

271
00:15:45,780 --> 00:15:51,040
The facts are, Marissa and I, after hours, we're in a handicapped bathroom together.

272
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:52,780
That's just true.

273
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:54,900
The facts are the facts, right?

274
00:15:54,900 --> 00:16:04,560
But those facts, apart from my perspective and my context of what actually happened, could be used to give people a false impression about something that's true with me.

275
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:20,040
And how often do we hear news, see news, share news that's one-sided, that causes somebody to look really poorly, and makes us look really good, and really it's just our perspective on the facts.

276
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:23,600
It's not necessarily false truth, it's shameful truth.

277
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:27,440
Or perhaps also partial truth, opinion.

278
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:29,660
It's click-baity, right?

279
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:32,520
It's like the headline of what we want people to hear.

280
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:38,460
And putting our own spin on things sometimes really does a lot of damage.

281
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:48,780
I think if we're honestly going to evaluate gospel before it comes out of our mouth, we should realize that most of the time it's extremely one-sided information from our perspective.

282
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:50,280
We're forsaking the golden rule.

283
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:52,580
We're not treating people the way that we would want to be treated.

284
00:16:52,580 --> 00:17:00,780
We also, we speak so much differently about people when they're not in the room.

285
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:05,440
And we speak so much differently about people that we have trust and relationship with.

286
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:09,140
We fill the gaps with love when we have a relationship with somebody.

287
00:17:09,140 --> 00:17:16,180
When it's a stranger, somebody that we don't yet know, or somebody that we're not fully trusting, we tend to fill the gaps with question marks.

288
00:17:16,180 --> 00:17:18,120
And we assume the worst about people.

289
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:34,080
And this is why I think this is so important for both of our congregations to get, because what's going to happen in this church is when we step on each other's toes, is you're going to tend to extend grace to the people that were a part of your former congregation, because you have relational equity with them, because you have trust.

290
00:17:34,460 --> 00:17:39,620
And you're going to say, oh, they didn't mean that that way, or what they did when they did that, they were thinking about this.

291
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:42,220
And what's going to happen when the other congregation does something?

292
00:17:42,220 --> 00:17:43,660
And you're going to be like, how dare them?

293
00:17:43,660 --> 00:17:46,600
Can you believe that other congregation?

294
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:49,340
They came in, and they did this thing, and they felt this way.

295
00:17:49,340 --> 00:18:08,060
And before we know it, if we don't fill the gaps with trust, if we don't choose to believe the best about other people, if we don't choose and refuse to speak behind people's backs, it's going to happen really quickly, where disunity is going to form between the two congregations, and we're going to have disunity where there shouldn't be.

296
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:11,820
Cossip is sharing bad news, false truth, shameful truth, partial truth.

297
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:17,580
Proverbs 19.5 says, a false witness will not go unpunished, nor will a liar escape.

298
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:20,420
This is really important stuff.

299
00:18:20,420 --> 00:18:23,660
So, sharing bad news behind someone's back.

300
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:26,300
This is a pretty self-explanatory part of the definition.

301
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:32,540
We usually understand that this is wrong, but we tend to justify it.

302
00:18:32,540 --> 00:18:40,240
Speaking behind someone's back makes us oftentimes a liar and a fool.

303
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:43,660
Proverbs 10.18 says,

304
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:49,620
Hiding hatred makes you a liar, and whoever others slander is a fool.

305
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:53,540
So, it makes us a liar because, again, it's one-sided truth.

306
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:57,600
We're not having the opportunity for the other person involved to share their perspective.

307
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:59,820
So, we're only sharing partial truth.

308
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:12,060
And it makes us a fool because we don't have an opportunity to learn, to grow, to build, and find unity in the midst of this difficult situation.

309
00:19:12,060 --> 00:19:16,540
So, bad news behind someone's back, out of a bad heart.

310
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:19,840
This is the part of the definition that we're best at justifying.

311
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:23,400
We could really justify that we're doing it for a good reason.

312
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:28,820
That this information that we're about to share is a good thing for us to share.

313
00:19:28,820 --> 00:19:32,920
We convince ourselves that we don't have ugly motivation for sharing the information.

314
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:40,120
If we're going to really spot this, if we're going to spot ourselves sharing bad news out of a bad heart behind someone's back,

315
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:45,140
I would say we have to ask ourselves, what value will sharing this add to my life?

316
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:50,860
What value will sharing this add to the person I'm speaking to?

317
00:19:50,860 --> 00:19:55,680
And what value will this add to the person I'm speaking about?

318
00:19:55,680 --> 00:20:01,640
Is this information adding value to my relationship, to the relationship with the brother and sister I'm talking to,

319
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:04,900
to the brother and sister I'm talking about?

320
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:07,540
Proverbs 16, 28 says,

321
00:20:07,540 --> 00:20:11,260
A gossip separates best friends.

322
00:20:11,260 --> 00:20:19,100
All it takes is one piece of partial information shared from a perspective that isn't a full perspective

323
00:20:19,100 --> 00:20:23,980
to really ruin some friendships and relationships.

324
00:20:23,980 --> 00:20:27,320
Proverbs 18, verse 2 says,

325
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:29,840
Fools have no interest in understanding.

326
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:33,820
They only want to air their own opinions.

327
00:20:35,020 --> 00:20:39,840
Gossip allows us to confirm our distorted perspectives of a situation.

328
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:45,920
I heard a pastor a few years ago say that if you ever find yourself saying the statement,

329
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:47,120
I don't understand,

330
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:50,900
that that's a you problem, not another person problem.

331
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:56,000
Like if you see a brother and sister in Christ doing something and you want to gossip behind them,

332
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,240
because you're like, how could they say that that way?

333
00:20:58,240 --> 00:20:59,080
How could they think that?

334
00:20:59,080 --> 00:20:59,920
How could they believe that?

335
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:01,100
How could they do that?

336
00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:04,020
The problem is not with them.

337
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:04,880
It's with you.

338
00:21:04,880 --> 00:21:08,740
And so the right thing to do is to go to that person and say, help me understand.

339
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:12,700
Help me understand how this is good or right.

340
00:21:12,700 --> 00:21:15,240
But instead, when we go behind their backs,

341
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:19,780
we confirm our own distorted opinions.

342
00:21:19,780 --> 00:21:25,240
The best way I can think to illustrate this is I like to spend some time at the cigar shop.

343
00:21:25,540 --> 00:21:28,180
The reason I spend some time at the cigar shop is, one, because I like cigars.

344
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:35,320
But two, because as a pastor, I often don't get a lot of time to spend with people that aren't inside the church.

345
00:21:35,320 --> 00:21:38,900
Like a huge majority of my time is with people inside the church.

346
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:40,800
And I just think that that could be a problem.

347
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:51,400
And so this is a great way for me to meet people with different perspectives and have conversations about Christianity and religion and politics and all the things that are happening.

348
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:58,000
And cigar shop is really interesting because you have people on, like, polar opposite perspectives in the room.

349
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:04,660
Like you have as far left MSNBC people and as far right Fox only people.

350
00:22:05,420 --> 00:22:10,820
And here's what happens with those two groups of people is they only listen to one perspective.

351
00:22:10,820 --> 00:22:12,320
Like, only.

352
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:16,400
Like, all they do is eat and consume one side perspective.

353
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:17,120
Either side.

354
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:18,860
They both do the same thing.

355
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:20,540
They both come to the same conclusions.

356
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:28,800
And then they sit on opposite sides of the room yelling back and forth to one another without actually listening because they've already confirmed their opinion.

357
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:33,820
They've listened to the news behind the other person's back, not hearing their perspective,

358
00:22:33,820 --> 00:22:36,940
and confirming the perspective that they already believe to be true.

359
00:22:36,940 --> 00:22:42,660
And it forces this wedge in between one another and causes disunity.

360
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:49,860
Or there's people like me who try and listen to both perspectives separately, right?

361
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:52,620
Like I listen to one perspective and then I listen to the other perspective.

362
00:22:52,620 --> 00:22:55,120
And they're both saying the same things and coming to different conclusions.

363
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:58,840
And so then I'm just skeptical of the entire process, right?

364
00:22:59,060 --> 00:23:01,480
So I think there's all of this stuff happening.

365
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:06,420
And the best thing that we can do to fix it as a body of Christ is to come together.

366
00:23:06,420 --> 00:23:12,360
The best thing that we can do is to work through these difficult conversations in love.

367
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:21,400
Because, like last week, if we have Jesus at the center, then we can really get into the messy stuff and keep him as a priority.

368
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:25,660
So, gossip often confirms our own distorted opinion.

369
00:23:25,660 --> 00:23:30,700
If you've gotten this far and you haven't felt convicted, remember, equal opportunity offender.

370
00:23:30,700 --> 00:23:36,720
Not only is speaking bearing bad news behind someone's back out of a bad heart gossip,

371
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:42,140
but so is listening to bad news behind someone's back out of a bad heart.

372
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:46,680
See, this is another one that we get really good at justifying.

373
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:47,220
It's not me.

374
00:23:47,220 --> 00:23:48,840
I'm not gossiping.

375
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:55,040
I'm just, you know, I'm just enjoying the thing that's going on here.

376
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:57,500
Proverbs 17, 4 says,

377
00:23:57,500 --> 00:23:59,800
Wrongdoers eagerly listen to gossip.

378
00:23:59,800 --> 00:24:03,620
Liars pay close attention to slander.

379
00:24:03,620 --> 00:24:05,880
Listening to gossip seems less bad,

380
00:24:05,880 --> 00:24:11,940
but it causes just as much damage in our heart and in our relationships as speaking gossip.

381
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:18,640
What comes into your ears and your mind will shape your heart.

382
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:24,100
This is why the Scripture commands us to take every thought captive and make it obedient to Jesus.

383
00:24:24,100 --> 00:24:27,980
But oftentimes, we're passive listeners,

384
00:24:27,980 --> 00:24:32,200
and we find ourselves just naturally, like, participating in this thing

385
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:36,540
where it's causing our hearts to feel something that they shouldn't be feeling.

386
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:43,760
It's ultimately sowing seeds of disunity that's going to separate us rather than unite us.

387
00:24:43,760 --> 00:24:47,140
It's breaking relationships unnecessarily.

388
00:24:47,140 --> 00:24:53,800
So we've got to be intentional to listen to other perspectives.

389
00:24:53,800 --> 00:24:59,380
I find myself as a pastor, oftentimes I'm in two separate rooms

390
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:05,020
where two separate groups of people are gossiping about the same exact thing from their perspective.

391
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:06,060
Right?

392
00:25:06,060 --> 00:25:10,240
And it's like, and again, this can happen with the two congregations coming together.

393
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:11,940
It's like, I'll sit in a room with some people from Grace,

394
00:25:11,940 --> 00:25:13,640
and they'll be like, well, this and this and this and this.

395
00:25:13,640 --> 00:25:16,360
And then I'll sit in a room with people from Camus.

396
00:25:16,360 --> 00:25:17,900
I'll be like, well, this and this and this and this.

397
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:22,700
And they're like, as a pastor, I'm like, man, like, imagine if this conversation was just happening together.

398
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:26,940
Like, because you're both, like, you're both so passionate about the thing that matters most,

399
00:25:26,940 --> 00:25:31,980
but you're kind of missing and filling in gaps because you haven't sat in the room together.

400
00:25:31,980 --> 00:25:34,620
And that's what the next couple of months is going to be about for us as a church,

401
00:25:34,620 --> 00:25:38,740
is like getting us together in the room and, like, understanding each other's perspectives

402
00:25:38,740 --> 00:25:42,260
and keeping Jesus as the primary focus.

403
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:51,700
So, here's a test for us to spot gossip, a couple questions that we can ask before we share.

404
00:25:51,700 --> 00:25:58,060
Number one, would I say this with this attitude as the person I'm talking about is in the room?

405
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:03,620
Again, sometimes we'd be like, yeah, I would say this, but would you say it the way that you're saying it?

406
00:26:03,620 --> 00:26:04,800
Right?

407
00:26:04,800 --> 00:26:09,580
Like, we tend to add inflection and add, like, comedic relief,

408
00:26:09,580 --> 00:26:12,900
and we talk about people in a way we would never talk about them if they're in the room.

409
00:26:12,980 --> 00:26:16,440
If your answer to that question is no, then it's gossip.

410
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:21,300
Would I react this way if the person that was being talked about was in the room?

411
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:27,480
Often do other people share gossip with us, and we're like, we lean in, and we're like, oh, my goodness.

412
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:29,060
They did what?

413
00:26:29,060 --> 00:26:31,040
They said they were wearing what?

414
00:26:31,040 --> 00:26:32,360
He went to the cigar shop.

415
00:26:32,360 --> 00:26:34,940
Oh, my gosh.

416
00:26:34,940 --> 00:26:36,000
Can you believe that?

417
00:26:36,440 --> 00:26:38,460
So, would I react this way?

418
00:26:38,460 --> 00:26:42,320
If the answer is no, then you're probably listening to gossip.

419
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:47,700
Would I want this conversation to happen this way if the information was about me?

420
00:26:49,620 --> 00:26:56,420
How often do we kind of dehumanize the person that's being talked about in the moment because it feels good,

421
00:26:56,420 --> 00:26:59,460
because it makes us feel better about the situation?

422
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:02,740
Gossip is bearing bad news behind someone's back out of a bad heart.

423
00:27:02,740 --> 00:27:07,600
It's not just about what we say, but about how we say it and why we say it.

424
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:18,360
Matthew 12, 36, and I tell you this, you must give an account on Judgment Day for every idle word you speak.

425
00:27:18,360 --> 00:27:28,140
Christians, if we counted our calories like we counted our words, most of us would be on my 600-pound life.

426
00:27:28,140 --> 00:27:35,940
Guys, I wrote that joke like a long time ago, and I was really hoping it was going to land better than that.

427
00:27:37,900 --> 00:27:39,620
It crashed, crashed and burned hard.

428
00:27:39,620 --> 00:27:45,140
No, but if we counted our words like we counted our calories like we counted our words,

429
00:27:45,140 --> 00:27:48,620
because most of us are pretty conscious, at least, of what we're eating.

430
00:27:48,620 --> 00:27:54,580
Even if we choose not to actually make the decision, we know what's going into our bodies.

431
00:27:54,580 --> 00:28:01,960
But far too often, we don't pay too much attention to the words that are being shared that we're hearing or that we're listening to.

432
00:28:02,680 --> 00:28:08,440
So, hopefully, by this point, you can acknowledge that this is a bad thing.

433
00:28:08,440 --> 00:28:09,880
So, how do we stop it?

434
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:13,260
If this is how we spot gossip, how do we stop gossip?

435
00:28:13,260 --> 00:28:18,840
Matthew 12, a couple verses before, verses 34 and 35, it says,

436
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:33,000
A good person produces good things from the treasury of a good heart, and an evil person produces evil things from the treasury of an evil heart.

437
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,820
Our words are controlled by our hearts.

438
00:28:35,860 --> 00:28:44,500
And what happens when we hear messages about gossip, when we feel conviction about gossip, if you're anything like me, is you just try and stop it.

439
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:47,760
All right, I know it's bad, I shouldn't do it, I'm going to stop it.

440
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:55,940
But here's the point, when you're trying to stop gossip, you're trying to tame your tongue, and James tells us that that's impossible.

441
00:28:55,940 --> 00:29:00,140
Like, you're never going to be able to succeed at taming your tongue.

442
00:29:00,140 --> 00:29:09,360
If you want to tame your tongue, you have to go at the root, you have to go to your heart, you have to find out what is the motivation behind why I am sharing this information.

443
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:13,940
And you have to start there, because if you start with your words, it won't last long.

444
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:16,600
You have to start with your heart.

445
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:22,920
So I want to go through a couple of heart motivations that I believe lead us to gossip most often.

446
00:29:22,920 --> 00:29:25,480
The first, I would say, is insecurity.

447
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:33,280
If you're insecure about who God has created you to be, about your strengths, about the person that God's called you to be, your identity,

448
00:29:33,280 --> 00:29:41,660
what you will tend to do is instead of going to God and allowing him to remind you that he sent his one and only son to die for you,

449
00:29:41,660 --> 00:29:48,820
that you are of utmost importance and value, and that he's created you uniquely and on purpose for a specific reason and a specific time,

450
00:29:48,820 --> 00:29:53,180
he's placed you where you are for a reason, and if you'd let him remind you of that,

451
00:29:53,180 --> 00:29:58,760
you wouldn't have to look at other people and compare yourself, because you are exactly who God made you to be.

452
00:29:59,380 --> 00:30:05,480
But instead of that, it's a lot easier for us to, like, this is what I do as a pastor or as a husband,

453
00:30:05,480 --> 00:30:12,580
is when I'm feeling insecure as a pastor, it's really easy to go on social media and find pastors that do things that I don't approve of,

454
00:30:12,580 --> 00:30:17,760
and then behind their backs tell all of my friends about how they're not as good of a pastor as I am.

455
00:30:17,760 --> 00:30:19,720
I would have never have done that.

456
00:30:19,720 --> 00:30:20,760
I never would have said that.

457
00:30:20,760 --> 00:30:22,280
I never would have allowed that thing to happen.

458
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:25,120
Where's that coming from?

459
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:27,160
Insecurity.

460
00:30:27,500 --> 00:30:33,880
Like, it's an evil thing in my heart, and when I can point that out, when I'm just addressing, like, the words, it's really easy to justify.

461
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:35,180
It's not that big a deal.

462
00:30:35,180 --> 00:30:39,240
But when you call out the heart motivation, you see how ugly it really is,

463
00:30:39,240 --> 00:30:44,420
and you see how much you're missing out on who God really has called you to be.

464
00:30:44,420 --> 00:30:45,700
I do this as a husband, too.

465
00:30:45,700 --> 00:30:50,300
Like, when I'm feeling insecure, I'm feeling like I'm just not measuring up to the husband that I'm called to be.

466
00:30:50,620 --> 00:30:57,540
How often do I just find somebody that does something that I would never do and be like, well, at least, I would say at least I don't play golf every week, but I do.

467
00:30:57,540 --> 00:30:58,760
So I can't do that.

468
00:30:58,760 --> 00:31:00,780
Like, well, at least I'm not out golfing all the time.

469
00:31:00,780 --> 00:31:02,180
Shoot, that's me.

470
00:31:03,460 --> 00:31:06,260
Yeah, but I can find other things that husbands do that I wouldn't do.

471
00:31:06,260 --> 00:31:16,880
And so, man, if we're going to address that type of gossip, we've got to get to the core of our identity of who Jesus has made us to be and remind ourselves of this.

472
00:31:16,920 --> 00:31:20,060
This was the biggest lesson that I learned on my sabbatical in August.

473
00:31:20,060 --> 00:31:25,500
Like, I was gone for an entire month, and Canvas was breaking attendance records while I was gone, right?

474
00:31:25,500 --> 00:31:28,780
And, like, what a reminder that it's not about me.

475
00:31:28,780 --> 00:31:31,860
Like, God's going to build his church.

476
00:31:31,860 --> 00:31:35,920
And he invites me to participate in it, but it's not about me.

477
00:31:35,920 --> 00:31:41,440
And all throughout sabbatical, I was listening to this song called Jaira, and there's a line in there that says,

478
00:31:41,440 --> 00:31:44,180
I've never been more loved than I am right now.

479
00:31:45,460 --> 00:31:51,420
And what a reminder for me that, like, in my insecurity, when I feel like I'm not measuring up as a husband,

480
00:31:51,420 --> 00:31:55,540
when I feel like I'm not measuring up as a pastor, when I feel like I'm not measuring up as a friend,

481
00:31:55,540 --> 00:31:59,840
man, God loves you so much right now.

482
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:02,120
He created you to be the person that you are.

483
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:08,340
And, like, yes, you're fallen and broken and sinful, but you don't have to tear other people down to build yourself up.

484
00:32:08,340 --> 00:32:14,360
You have all of the security you need in your relationship as a son or daughter of a king.

485
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:16,860
So, insecurity.

486
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:18,880
The second is revenge.

487
00:32:18,880 --> 00:32:21,500
Hurt people tend to hurt people.

488
00:32:21,500 --> 00:32:22,420
You've heard this phrase?

489
00:32:22,420 --> 00:32:32,800
And so what happens is when you get hurt by somebody, what's easier than trying to go to them and work out their relationship is it's easier for you to be passive-aggressive behind their back

490
00:32:32,800 --> 00:32:38,300
And to use your words to hurt them the way that they hurt you.

491
00:32:38,300 --> 00:32:40,580
And this is something that I've struggled with so much.

492
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:55,120
When somebody that you love deeply hurts you deeply, it's so easy to start this rumor mill to go behind their back to justify your feelings to other people.

493
00:32:56,120 --> 00:32:57,780
It doesn't add value to your life.

494
00:32:57,780 --> 00:33:00,900
It doesn't add value to the life of the person that you're talking to.

495
00:33:00,900 --> 00:33:03,880
And it doesn't add value to the life of the person you're talking about.

496
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:07,900
So, revenge is often a motivation.

497
00:33:07,900 --> 00:33:10,220
I love what Paul says in Romans chapter 12.

498
00:33:10,220 --> 00:33:13,220
He says, never repay evil with more evil.

499
00:33:13,220 --> 00:33:15,060
Do all that you can.

500
00:33:15,060 --> 00:33:17,940
Do all that you can to live at peace with everyone.

501
00:33:17,940 --> 00:33:19,720
Never take revenge.

502
00:33:19,720 --> 00:33:22,900
Leave that to the righteous anger of God.

503
00:33:22,980 --> 00:33:26,560
As far as it depends on you, live at peace with all people.

504
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:35,480
So, when this issue arises in our hearts, what we should do is we should remind ourselves that God is a just God.

505
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:38,480
And that I don't need to repay evil for evil.

506
00:33:38,480 --> 00:33:40,460
Like, God is the judge of everything.

507
00:33:40,460 --> 00:33:42,880
I need to trust Him to do it.

508
00:33:42,880 --> 00:33:45,640
A couple more motivations.

509
00:33:45,640 --> 00:33:47,860
I'm running late, so I'm going to try and rush through these.

510
00:33:47,860 --> 00:33:48,480
Fear of man.

511
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:52,900
This is the main motivation behind listening to gossip.

512
00:33:53,720 --> 00:33:54,580
Fear of man.

513
00:33:54,580 --> 00:33:59,280
We just want to fit in with the group, so we go along with whatever is being said.

514
00:33:59,280 --> 00:34:07,820
Proverbs 29, verse 25 says, fearing people is a dangerous trap, but trusting in the Lord means safety.

515
00:34:07,820 --> 00:34:11,680
Don't be afraid to step out in a situation and say, should we really be talking about this right now?

516
00:34:11,680 --> 00:34:15,980
Have you talked to this person like speaking the truth in love?

517
00:34:15,980 --> 00:34:20,520
Let's allow ourselves to keep our eyes on Jesus and not the fear of other people.

518
00:34:20,520 --> 00:34:21,740
Lastly, boredom.

519
00:34:21,740 --> 00:34:22,700
Anybody?

520
00:34:23,420 --> 00:34:25,480
Seen the tabloids right recently.

521
00:34:25,480 --> 00:34:28,500
When you're bored, you will find things to talk about.

522
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:35,820
Second Thessalonians, chapter 3, verse 11 says, we hear, Paul, we hear that some of you are living idle lives.

523
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:36,420
You're not working.

524
00:34:36,420 --> 00:34:37,240
You don't have a job.

525
00:34:37,240 --> 00:34:41,080
And so, instead of working, you meddle in other people's business.

526
00:34:41,080 --> 00:34:43,020
This is our heart posture.

527
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:47,860
When we don't have enough things to do, we look for things to entertain.

528
00:34:47,860 --> 00:34:51,440
How often do we find ourselves hanging out with friends with nothing to talk about?

529
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:55,600
We're like, oh, I have a good story to tell you about this thing that happened with this person.

530
00:34:55,600 --> 00:34:58,600
And it might add value to that conversation.

531
00:34:58,600 --> 00:35:01,640
It might add entertainment, but it doesn't add value.

532
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:14,880
So, as we look to the motivation of gossip in our lives, we should ask ourselves, what about my identity in Christ can put this sinful motivation to death in my life?

533
00:35:16,640 --> 00:35:22,140
If you're a follower of Jesus, you don't have the opportunity to be bored.

534
00:35:22,140 --> 00:35:23,240
There's too much to do.

535
00:35:23,240 --> 00:35:27,000
Second Corinthians 5, verse 20 says, we are Christ's ambassadors.

536
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:29,340
God is making his appeal through us.

537
00:35:29,340 --> 00:35:31,580
We speak for Christ.

538
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:35,940
We speak for Christ when we plead, come back to God.

539
00:35:35,940 --> 00:35:38,200
We are Christ's ambassadors.

540
00:35:38,200 --> 00:35:43,340
What comes out of our mouth is a representation of the king of our lives.

541
00:35:43,340 --> 00:35:45,680
This is important.

542
00:35:47,440 --> 00:35:51,280
So, does this mean you can never say anything behind anyone's back ever?

543
00:35:51,280 --> 00:35:53,580
No.

544
00:35:53,580 --> 00:35:54,920
Right.

545
00:35:54,920 --> 00:35:55,400
Good job.

546
00:35:55,400 --> 00:35:56,080
Nice and proud of you.

547
00:35:56,080 --> 00:36:02,180
There is a way to share bad news behind someone's back out of a good heart.

548
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:04,300
But it's really difficult.

549
00:36:04,300 --> 00:36:07,580
And you have to be intentional to do it rightly.

550
00:36:07,580 --> 00:36:10,340
Ephesians 4, verse 29.

551
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:11,860
We referenced this earlier.

552
00:36:11,860 --> 00:36:18,760
But Paul says, let everything you say be good and helpful so that your words will be an encouragement.

553
00:36:18,760 --> 00:36:27,240
If you're seeking wisdom from a mentor or a friend, if you're going to get some piece of information that might clarify the thing that you're confused about,

554
00:36:27,860 --> 00:36:34,060
if you're doing it in a good and helpful way, it's possible that you can have some of these conversations.

555
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:36,920
But it should always lead you to do one of two things.

556
00:36:36,920 --> 00:36:45,300
It should always lead you to either go to the person and have a clarifying conversation or to go to God and get over it.

557
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:50,380
Like, there are certain things that really we need to go to the person to have a conversation about.

558
00:36:50,380 --> 00:36:56,180
And there are certain things that, like, we're just spun up over something that really isn't that important in the grand scheme of things.

559
00:36:56,180 --> 00:37:01,160
And we can go to God and say, God, like, I do this with the hurt people thing.

560
00:37:01,160 --> 00:37:07,240
Like, when revenge is in my heart, I go to God and I'm like, God, like, man, do I want this person to feel my wrath.

561
00:37:07,240 --> 00:37:11,780
Man, do I want to go behind their backs and tell people everything that they did to me.

562
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:22,880
In that situation, I can either go to them or I can go to God and say, I, it's not up to me.

563
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:24,940
You are just.

564
00:37:24,940 --> 00:37:28,740
I am just going to be obedient to you.

565
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:34,680
So, if it's bad, don't say it unless you're willing to do something about it.

566
00:37:34,680 --> 00:37:39,560
It's okay to share with a mentor or friend when you're wrestling through to get good advice.

567
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:44,940
Let's do it out of sincere heart and then either approach the person or give it to God in prayer.

568
00:37:44,940 --> 00:37:49,180
This is literally what Paul talked about last week, speaking the truth in love.

569
00:37:49,180 --> 00:37:53,660
Gossip is way easier than speaking the truth in love in the moment.

570
00:37:53,660 --> 00:38:00,240
It feels better in the moment, but it's a cheap substitute for speaking the truth in love.

571
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:05,880
It might scratch a momentary itch, but it will never provide lasting relief.

572
00:38:06,880 --> 00:38:16,200
Imagine a church full of people who refuse to gossip and slander, who instead intentionally speak the truth in love.

573
00:38:17,860 --> 00:38:20,220
First Peter, I told you we'd get there.

574
00:38:20,220 --> 00:38:21,400
I'm only eight minutes late.

575
00:38:21,400 --> 00:38:26,180
First Peter, chapter 2, verses 21 and on.

576
00:38:26,180 --> 00:38:37,080
For God called you to do good, even if it means suffering, just as Christ suffered for you.

577
00:38:37,340 --> 00:38:43,020
Jesus is your example, and you must follow in his footsteps.

578
00:38:43,020 --> 00:38:49,540
He never sinned, nor was deceit found in his mouth.

579
00:38:49,540 --> 00:38:55,240
He did not retaliate when he was insulted, nor threaten revenge when he suffered.

580
00:38:55,240 --> 00:39:00,160
He left his case in the hands of God, who always judges fairly.

581
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:10,140
He personally carried your sin and my sin in his body on the cross, so that we can be dead to sin and live for what is right.

582
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:12,960
By his wounds, you are healed.

583
00:39:14,420 --> 00:39:20,360
Once you were like sheep who wandered away, but now you have turned to your shepherd, the guardian of your soul.

584
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:28,860
If you are in here and you are a follower of Jesus, you want something to motivate your heart, to kill gossip in your life?

585
00:39:28,860 --> 00:39:29,460
It's Christ.

586
00:39:29,460 --> 00:39:31,600
He is your perfect example.

587
00:39:31,780 --> 00:39:36,900
If anyone could have justified speaking bad news behind someone's back, it's Jesus.

588
00:39:36,900 --> 00:39:46,300
As he was going to the cross, those whom he created, those whom he put breath in their lungs, those whom he was sustaining with his power,

589
00:39:46,300 --> 00:39:50,620
as he was going to the cross, they were spitting on him and mocking him.

590
00:39:50,620 --> 00:39:51,820
And what was his response?

591
00:39:51,820 --> 00:39:56,260
Forgive them, for they know not what they do.

592
00:39:59,380 --> 00:40:09,020
When we're tempted to gossip, when we're tempted to slander, we remind ourselves of the perfect example that we have in Jesus.

593
00:40:09,020 --> 00:40:20,420
He went ahead of us, showed us the way, bought us redemption and forgiveness, so that we don't have to get revenge for ourselves,

594
00:40:20,420 --> 00:40:22,300
so we don't have to get justice for ourselves.

595
00:40:22,300 --> 00:40:25,520
Jesus secured justice on the cross.

596
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:28,580
And one day justice will happen.

597
00:40:28,580 --> 00:40:30,400
And it's not our job.

598
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:33,280
Our job is to follow him.

599
00:40:33,280 --> 00:40:39,220
So, if you're in here and you're not a follower of Jesus, I tell you this.

600
00:40:39,220 --> 00:40:43,720
This is one of the most practical messages that you will ever hear at church.

601
00:40:43,720 --> 00:40:46,860
Because you can go home and you can put all of these principles into practice.

602
00:40:46,860 --> 00:40:48,320
You can not gossip.

603
00:40:48,320 --> 00:40:49,180
You can not slander.

604
00:40:49,180 --> 00:40:51,480
And your relationships will be a lot better.

605
00:40:51,480 --> 00:40:53,620
Like, your life will just get easier.

606
00:40:55,580 --> 00:41:00,220
But it's woefully short of what Jesus died to provide you.

607
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:05,260
He didn't go to the cross just to give you, like, smoother relationships.

608
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:08,360
He went to the cross to pay for your sin.

609
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:09,160
I would say it this way.

610
00:41:09,160 --> 00:41:12,940
Wisdom for life cannot compare to a hope for the future.

611
00:41:12,940 --> 00:41:15,400
So, like, this practical advice.

612
00:41:15,400 --> 00:41:16,660
You could read the book of Proverbs.

613
00:41:16,660 --> 00:41:18,360
You could put a lot of these things into practice.

614
00:41:18,360 --> 00:41:22,900
Maybe some of you are here because you're just looking for, like, good life wisdom and advice.

615
00:41:23,020 --> 00:41:24,220
There's a lot of it in Scripture.

616
00:41:24,220 --> 00:41:26,960
And it will add some value to your life.

617
00:41:26,960 --> 00:41:29,940
But it's not what this whole thing is about.

618
00:41:29,940 --> 00:41:37,100
This whole thing is about, what this whole thing is about is the broken body of Christ for us, the dead blood of Christ for us.

619
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:38,840
That our sin demands a punishment.

620
00:41:38,840 --> 00:41:40,880
That we can never live up to.

621
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:43,140
And that Jesus came in our place for our sins.

622
00:41:43,140 --> 00:41:49,280
So, if you're here and you're not sure what that looks like in your life, then, like, have a conversation with somebody that you came with.

623
00:41:49,280 --> 00:41:50,140
Or come talk to me.

624
00:41:50,140 --> 00:41:51,840
I'd love to walk you through it.

625
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:57,300
So, I'm going to pray, and then we're going to take communion together.

626
00:41:57,300 --> 00:42:03,600
Father, we are grateful for this reminder that Jesus is our perfect example.

627
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:10,000
That in every way he was tempted, and yet he did not sin.

628
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:12,480
He showed us how to live.

629
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:17,340
And we want to imitate that, God.

630
00:42:17,340 --> 00:42:19,860
We want to become more like him on a daily basis.

631
00:42:19,860 --> 00:42:22,620
But we fall short over and over and over again.

632
00:42:22,620 --> 00:42:27,320
And Satan, in those moments, wants us to feel guilt and pain.

633
00:42:28,780 --> 00:42:30,580
But that's not why Jesus came to die.

634
00:42:30,580 --> 00:42:32,480
He came to give us grace and forgiveness.

635
00:42:32,480 --> 00:42:34,380
And so, may we receive that forgiveness.

636
00:42:34,380 --> 00:42:40,960
May we taste and see that the Lord is good and walk in for forgiveness of Jesus.

637
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:47,660
God, may we be a church that refuses to take the easy way out.

638
00:42:47,660 --> 00:42:50,380
That refuses to gossip and slander.

639
00:42:50,380 --> 00:42:54,180
But instead, either takes it to you and lets you be in charge.

640
00:42:54,180 --> 00:42:56,520
Or goes proactively to that person.

641
00:42:56,640 --> 00:43:02,780
So that we might grow more and more daily into the head of this church who is Jesus.

642
00:43:02,780 --> 00:43:05,180
For your glory and for our good.

643
00:43:05,180 --> 00:43:07,220
In Jesus' name I pray.
